    <section id="manifesto">
      <div class="narrow lede">
        <p class="kicker">01 — Opening</p>
        <p>The claim is specific. Quran 7:157 calls Muhammad the unlettered prophet whom the people of the Book find written in the Torah and the Gospel. The verse Muslims then open is Deuteronomy 18:18: a prophet like Moses, from among their brothers, with God’s words in his mouth. This page grants the appeal, for the length of the argument. Open the chapter they opened.</p>
<p>Three verses later the same speech names the false prophet. He is the man who speaks in God’s name a word God did not command, or who speaks in the name of other gods. The early Muslim history, not a Christian pamphlet, says Muhammad recited praise of al-Lat, al-Uzza, and Manat, then said he had fabricated against God. If that report is history, Deuteronomy 18:20 has already classified him. If that report is thrown out, the biblical announcement they wanted goes with the standard they refuse.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kicker">Deuteronomy 18:20</p>
        <blockquote>But the prophet, which shall presume to speak a word in my name, which I have not commanded him to speak, or that shall speak in the name of other gods, even that prophet shall die.</blockquote>
        <p class="fine">King James Version. Verses 18 and 19, which Muslim writers treat as the announcement, promise a prophet who speaks what God commands. Verse 20 is the penalty for the two failures: an uncommanded word in God’s name, or speech in the name of other gods. Verse 22 adds a third public test: if the word does not happen, God did not speak it. This page does not turn the death penalty into a present instruction.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
