        <div class="narrow" style="margin:0">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h2>Two deductions. Both use their premises.</h2>
          <p class="deck">The form is old and valid. If the statute defines a false prophet this way, and the report says he did this, the classification follows. The fight is about the premises, so the premises are stated where a reader can check them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-grid"><article class="claim">
        <p class="num">01</p>
        <h3>The uncommanded word</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The defense</p>
        <p>A true prophet may recite a sentence God did not give him, provided God later withdraws it.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The text</p>
        <p>Deuteronomy 18:20 does not say that. It says the prophet who speaks a word in God’s name which God has not commanded shall die. Withdrawal is not the test. The speaking is the test. In al-Tabari’s report the sentence was spoken as revelation, in a surah, to a crowd.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">02</p>
        <h3>The names of other gods</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The defense</p>
        <p>Mentioning al-Lat, al-Uzza, and Manat was only a quotation of what the Meccans believed.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The text</p>
        <p>The canonical Quran does quote them, and then condemns them. Quran 53:19–23 calls them names you and your fathers invented, with no authority. The sira’s extra lines do the opposite. Tabari, volume VI, page 111: “These are the high flying cranes, verily their intercession is to be desired.” Page 110 has the other wording, “whose intercession was accepted with approval.” Intercession accepted is worship, not a footnote.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">03</p>
        <h3>His own sentence</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The defense</p>
        <p>The line about fabricating against God is a Christian cut-and-paste, or a regret that proves he was sincere.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The text</p>
        <p>The sentence is on the page. The History of al-Tabari, volume VI, page 111, SUNY translation: “I have fabricated things against God and have imputed to Him words which He has not spoken.” Tabari gives a chain. He is not swearing the report is sound. In the story, Gabriel has just said he did not bring those two phrases. A regret is still a confession that the words went out as God’s.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">04</p>
        <h3>Quran 22:52 as the rescue</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The defense</p>
        <p>God promised that Satan would cast something into every prophet’s recitation, and that God would cancel it. So the episode, if it happened, is a mark of prophethood.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The text</p>
        <p>Quran 22:52 does say that no messenger was sent except that Satan threw something into his recitation, and that God abolishes what Satan throws and confirms his own signs. Translators differ on the verb. The verse never names the three goddesses. What it cannot do is repeal Deuteronomy 18:20. A statute that condemns the uncommanded word is not satisfied by a later poem about cancellation.</p>
      </article></div>
