    <section>
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">The two proofs</p>
        <h2>If their premises stand, the classification is finished.</h2>
        <p>First. If a man speaks in the name of other gods, Deuteronomy 18:20 calls him a false prophet. The Tabari report says Muhammad recited, as revelation, that the intercession of al-Lat, al-Uzza, and Manat was to be desired. Those are other gods. Therefore, on that report, the statute’s name for him is false prophet.</p>
<p>Second. If a man delivers a word in God’s name that God did not command, the same verse classifies him the same way. The report says Gabriel denied the two phrases, and Muhammad answered that he had imputed to God words God had not spoken. Therefore, on that report, the statute’s name for him is false prophet a second time.</p>
<p>Neither proof needs a miracle, a battle, or a biography of Medina. Both are conditional on a Meccan report that Muslims preserved and that later Muslims denied. The condition is the whole argument. Hide the condition and the page becomes a slogan. State it, and the reader can throw the report out — and must then live with what throwing it out costs.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">04</p>
        <h2>The replies, and what each one abandons.</h2>
        <p>Reply one: Deuteronomy is corrupted, so verse 20 does not bind. Then verse 18 does not announce him either. Quran 7:157 said the Torah writes him. A corrupted Torah writes nothing a prophet can use. The appeal to Deuteronomy is over.</p>
<p>Reply two: the crane story is a forgery of heretics, as later scholars said, and Ibn Ishaq has been quoted to that effect. Then say the rest. The story sits in the early history with a chain. Muslims copied it for centuries. Bukhari still has pagans prostrating at Surat al-Najm, which the story was written to explain. Quran 22:52 still says Satan throws something into a prophet’s recitation. A forgery theory has to explain why the community kept the embarrassing report, not only why a critic likes it.</p>
<p>Reply three: it happened, and God corrected it, so he is more of a prophet, not less. That is Quran 22:52’s comfort, and it is not Deuteronomy 18:20’s standard. The Torah test they claimed is not “God patched it afterward.” The test is whether the word spoken in God’s name was God’s. By the story’s own ending, two of the words were not.</p>
      </div>
